ghpull:635 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "Typed error enum for SPARQL executor and notebook kernel" ;
    bl:number 635 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:460 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr635 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:460 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-09-03T19:44:12Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-08T15:40:08Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr635Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #460 (part of the error-handling epic #453) asked for the SPARQL executor's eval_path_pattern-and-friends and the notebook kernel's dispatch_cell to stop threading Result<_, String> and instead use a typed error enum, matching the existing pattern (UpdateError, AuthError) elsewhere in the workspace. Before writing any enum, I inventoried every place sparql_parser's executor actually *constructs* an error, expecting many call sites given how many functions across execute/*.rs return Result<_, String>. It turned out there are only three: two SERVICE-network-policy checks in execute/mod.rs::execute_inner, and Deadline::check's cooperative #372 timeout in deadline.rs. Every other Result<_, String> in the module (BGP/OPTIONAL/UNION/MINUS/GRAPH join chain, property-path evaluation) exists purely to propagate one of those three via `?` at deadline.check()? loop-iteration boundaries — so the fix is a compact three-variant ExecError (ServiceDenied, ServiceNotImplemented, Timeout), mechanically threaded through every Result<_, String> signature in execute/*.rs and deadline.rs, rather than a sprawling enum mirroring every function. For the kernel side, dispatch_cell itself constructs three distinguishable failure shapes (an unsafe-path rejection from check_path_safe, a file-open io::Error, and a message forwarded from whichever cell-type subsystem ran), so CellError got three matching variants (UnsafePath, Io, Execution(String)). I deliberately left Execution as a String-carrying catch-all rather than typing every one of turtle/RML/Manchester/SHACL/OTTR/Datalog/reasoning/SPARQL's own errors individually — issue #460's stated scope is dispatch_cell and the SPARQL executor, and fully typing eight independent subsystems' error surfaces is a separate, much larger effort. While auditing downstream consumers of the old String errors I found the exact anti-pattern #460 complains about already metastasized two levels down: sparql_endpoint::query::query_execution_error_response and explain::explain_query_response both did message.contains(\"exceeded the configured timeout\") to pick between HTTP 503 and 500. Since ExecError now exists, I fixed both to match!(err, ExecError::Timeout) as part of this PR rather than filing it as a follow-up — it's the same root problem the issue is about, directly enabled by the new type, and touches the same call path. Downstream crates that still want a String contract (src/lib.rs::run_sparql_query, shacl::sparql_constraints, dagalog-kernel::cell::sparql::execute_sparql) got a `.map_err(|e| e.to_string())` rather than being converted, since widening their own error types was out of scope. Tests that previously asserted on error-message substrings (network_policy.rs, query_timeout_tests.rs, graph_execution_tests.rs, path_security_tests.rs) were updated to match on the typed variant instead, which is the whole point of the change. One real bug surfaced by CI (not local testing): a doc comment on CellError linked to `crate::sockets::dispatch_cell` as an intra-doc link, but dispatch_cell is a private fn and rustdoc under -D warnings couldn't resolve it from a different module's doc comment even with --document-private-items; fixed by making it plain text. Locally, cargo test --workspace repeatedly hit two categories of noise that turned out to be environment artifacts rather than real regressions: stale-rlib compile errors from other worktrees concurrently rebuilding the same shared CARGO_TARGET_DIR (resolved per this repo's documented hazard by touching the affected files and rebuilding), and an OOM SIGKILL on sparql_endpoint's slow_transitive_closure_query_times_out_with_503 test, which deliberately builds a 3,000,000-node graph sized for a ~16GB CI runner and cannot fit on this dev box's 3.7GB — confirmed as environment-only once CI ran the same test green." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:635 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr635 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr635Summary agp:decisionPoint session:pr635Decision .

session:pr635Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Scoped ExecError to exactly the three variants the executor actually constructs (ServiceDenied, ServiceNotImplemented, Timeout), threading the same enum through every Result<_, String> signature in execute/*.rs and deadline.rs via a mechanical `?`-propagation change, instead of inventing a larger enum with a variant per function or evaluation stage." ;
    agp:alternative "A finer-grained enum (e.g. separate variants per BGP/path/aggregate evaluation failure) was considered, since that's the more common shape for 'typed error' refactors. Rejected once the audit showed those functions never construct an error themselves — they only ever propagate the timeout via `?` — so extra variants would have no distinct construction site and would just be dead surface area matching against something that can never actually produce them." .
